## Try it

[Pinment is on GitHub](https://github.com/khawkins98/pinment) and the [bookmarklet hub is live](https://khawkins98.github.io/pinment/). Drag the bookmarklet to your bar and try it on any page. If a selector breaks, a pin lands in the wrong spot, or you hit the URL length ceiling sooner than expected -- [file an issue](https://github.com/khawkins98/pinment/issues).

It's not as bulletproof as marking up a PDF, but it's lighter, faster to set up, and nobody has to create an account to collaborate.

A browser extension would be more reliable on pages with strict Content Security Policy, and I may build one eventually -- but then everyone has to install it. The bookmarklet keeps the barrier at zero.

[Try it out](https://khawkins98.github.io/pinment/)

If nothing else, it's a chance to rediscover [bookmarklets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bookmarklet). They're a useful bit of web tech that's gone [unloved since peaking around 2011](https://trends.google.com/trends/explore?date=all&q=bookmarklet).

